From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:38:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] shaders/sampling: exact weights for an upscaling by a
 whole ratio

pl_shader_sample_ortho2() takes the weights of its taps from a LUT of
256 rows, one per subpixel offset from 0 to 1, interpolated linearly
between rows by the texture unit, at the offset that the shader computes
from its interpolated sample position. sh_lut() is not given a format,
so it takes the first float format with at least 16 bits that can be
filtered; on an OpenGL ES context, such as Kodi's on GBM, that is
rgba16f (asked of libplacebo on Mesa's OpenGL ES with the query that
sh_lut() makes). A half float has 11 significant bits, so each weight is
off by up to one part in 2048 of itself, and the shader's division by
the sum of the weights only takes out what the errors have in common.
At a sharp edge the result is off by up to about one code of 4095.

For the planes of a Dolby Vision picture the ratios are whole numbers:
the chroma of the base layer and the luma of a 1080p enhancement layer
are scaled by 2 in each direction, the chroma of that layer by 4. Output
pixel j of such an axis samples the source at rect.x0 + (j + 0.5) / R,
so its subpixel offset repeats every R pixels and the convolution needs
R rows of weights, not a continuum.

When one axis is scaled up by a whole ratio R from 2 to 8 and the other
one is 1:1 on whole texels, those R rows are now computed in double
precision from the filter itself, the way compute_row() computes a row
of the LUT (pl_filter_sample() at the distance of each tap from the
sample position, normalised to a sum of 1), and given to the shader as a
uniform array. The shader recovers the index of its output pixel along
the axis by rounding (sample position - origin) * R, which the error of
an interpolated position (well under 1/R) cannot move, and from it the
row and the first tap; each tap is fetched at the centre of its texel
with nearest sampling. Neither the row nor the taps depend on how
precisely the GPU interpolated the position or filters a texture, and
there is no LUT texture to fetch. The origin, the rows and the first
taps are uniforms: the text of the shader depends only on R, the number
of taps and the components, so a different crop or chroma siting does
not compile another program.

Everything else keeps the LUT: other ratios, downscaling, a scaled
axis whose other axis is not 1:1 on whole texels, a flipped axis, polar
filters, an external or rectangle sampler, GLSL before 1.30 or ESSL
before 3.00 (a uniform array indexed by a computed int), and filters
whose taps would not fit in 16 vec4 for R rows. Anti-ringing works as
before, on the two middle taps. The main scaler takes the new path as
well when it upscales by a whole ratio.

Measured on Mesa's software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe), in codes of
4095, against a double-precision computation of libplacebo's Lanczos on
the CPU:
- Planes of three profile 7 FEL pictures, as Kodi's Dolby Vision
  renderer has libplacebo scale them (the two passes drawn again into
  textures of their own and read back): before, up to 0.0025 (base
  chroma, 2x2), 0.046 (enhancement luma, 2x2) and 0.0089 (enhancement
  chroma, 4x4); after, up to 0.0009, 0.0010 and 0.0009, the float
  arithmetic of the two passes. The same pictures at 1080p: before up
  to 0.031, after up to 0.00095. A half-float LUT computed on the CPU
  at the exact offsets errs by up to 0.015 on the 2160p planes: the
  rest of the error before comes from the offset that the shader reads
  from the interpolated position (inferred, not measured apart).
- The main scaler of the bridge for a 1080p picture on a 2160p output
  (Lanczos on the PQ RGB of the reconstruction, no sigmoid, 32-bit
  intermediates), against the reference from its own input: before,
  up to 0.67; after, up to 0.0015. On the same input, the new build
  is nowhere more than 0.0005 further from the reference than before.
- Synthetic 16-bit planes with full-range edges, ratios 2 to 8, offsets
  of chroma sitings, of crops and that double cannot hold, one to three
  components, anti-ringing, one or both axes: before, up to 0.95; after,
  up to 0.0015 in all 15 cases. A ratio of 1.5, a ratio of 9 and rows
  half a texel off keep the LUT and give the same results as before.
- Whole renders of a synthetic 4:2:0 picture through libplacebo's
  renderer with 32-bit intermediates, against a double-precision
  computation of the whole chain (decode; at 2x for SDR linearize,
  sigmoidize, scale, unsigmoidize, delinearize; for HDR/PQ the scaling
  alone): before, up to 17.4 (SDR, 2x) and 1.03 (PQ, 2x); after, up to
  0.0105 and 0.0027, and nowhere more than 0.002 further than before.
  With libplacebo's default half-float intermediates (which this
  software renderer rounds toward zero) both builds are bound by that
  rounding; apart from the samples whose result changes when the
  stored values move by a millionth (those at a half-float step), the
  new build is again nowhere more than 0.001 further than before.
- The reconstructed picture of the three 2160p pictures (12-bit PQ
  RGB): 554 to 18126 pixels of 8294400 change, by one code (334 by
  two). The speed on a GPU is not measured.

License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, that of libplacebo and of the file changed,
whose notice stays as it is.
---
 src/shaders/sampling.c | 213 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 211 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/shaders/sampling.c b/src/shaders/sampling.c
--- a/src/shaders/sampling.c
+++ b/src/shaders/sampling.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 
 #include <math.h>
 #include "shaders.h"
+#include "filters.h"
 
 #include <libplacebo/colorspace.h>
 #include <libplacebo/shaders/sampling.h>
@@ -947,6 +948,202 @@ enum {
     SEP_PASSES
 };
 
+// Upscaling one axis by a whole ratio R, with the other axis 1:1 on whole
+// texels. Output pixel j of the scaled axis samples the source at
+// `rect.x0 + (j + 0.5) / R`, so its subpixel offset repeats every R pixels:
+// the convolution only ever needs R rows of weights. Those rows are computed
+// here in double precision from the filter itself, as compute_row() computes
+// the rows of the LUT, and given to the shader as uniforms, one row per
+// phase. The shader picks the phase and the first tap from the integer index
+// of its output pixel, which it recovers from the sample position by
+// rounding, so neither depends on how precisely the GPU interpolated that
+// position, and fetches each tap at the centre of its texel with nearest
+// sampling.
+//
+// The LUT path interpolates the weights between 256 rows of a texture of the
+// first float format with at least 16 bits that sh_lut() finds, normally a
+// half-float one, whose 11 significant bits leave each weight off by up to
+// about one part in 2048 (one code of 4095 at a sharp edge). It is kept for
+// every other case.
+#define ORTHO_EXACT_MAX_RATIO 8
+#define ORTHO_EXACT_MAX_VEC4  16
+
+struct ortho_exact {
+    int pass;       // the scaled axis: SEP_HORIZ or SEP_VERT
+    int ratio;      // the whole upscaling ratio along it
+    double origin;  // where output pixel 0 samples it, in texels, minus 0.5
+};
+
+static bool ortho_exact_plan(pl_shader sh, const struct pl_sample_src *src,
+                             const struct pl_filter_config *filter,
+                             struct ortho_exact *out)
+{
+    // A uniform array indexed by a computed int: GLSL 1.30, ESSL 3.00
+    const struct pl_glsl_version glsl = sh_glsl(sh);
+    if (glsl.version < (glsl.gles ? 300 : 130))
+        return false;
+    if (!src->tex || src->tex->sampler_type != PL_SAMPLER_NORMAL)
+        return false;
+    if (filter->polar || !filter->kernel || src->new_w <= 0 || src->new_h <= 0)
+        return false;
+
+    // The source rect as setup_src() takes it
+    const struct pl_tex_params *tp = &src->tex->params;
+    const double x0 = src->rect.x0, y0 = src->rect.y0;
+    double w = pl_rect_w(src->rect), h = pl_rect_h(src->rect);
+    w = w ? w : tp->w;
+    h = h ? h : tp->h;
+
+    const bool same_x = w == src->new_w && x0 == floor(x0);
+    const bool same_y = h == src->new_h && y0 == floor(y0);
+    double start, len;
+    int new_len;
+    if (same_x && !same_y) {
+        out->pass = SEP_VERT;
+        start = y0, len = h, new_len = src->new_h;
+    } else if (same_y && !same_x) {
+        out->pass = SEP_HORIZ;
+        start = x0, len = w, new_len = src->new_w;
+    } else {
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    if (!(len > 0.0))
+        return false;
+    const long ratio = lround(new_len / len);
+    if (ratio < 2 || ratio > ORTHO_EXACT_MAX_RATIO || ratio * len != new_len)
+        return false;
+
+    // The number of taps, bounded as pl_filter_generate() bounds it. An
+    // upscaling filter is not widened.
+    struct pl_filter_config cfg = *filter;
+    cfg.blur = PL_DEF(cfg.blur, 1.0f);
+    const float bound = pl_filter_radius_bound(&cfg);
+    if (!(bound > 0.0f) || bound > 64.0f)
+        return false;
+    const int taps = 2 * (int) ceilf(bound);
+    if (ratio * PL_DIV_UP(taps, 4) > ORTHO_EXACT_MAX_VEC4)
+        return false;
+
+    out->ratio = ratio;
+    out->origin = start + 0.5 / ratio - 0.5;
+    return true;
+}
+
+static bool ortho_exact_sample(pl_shader sh, pl_filter filt,
+                               const struct pl_filter_config *cfg,
+                               const struct ortho_exact *ex, uint8_t comps,
+                               float scale, ident_t src_tex, ident_t pos,
+                               ident_t pt)
+{
+    const int N = filt->row_size, R = ex->ratio, S = PL_DIV_UP(N, 4);
+    if (N < 2 || N % 2 || R * S > ORTHO_EXACT_MAX_VEC4) {
+        SH_FAIL(sh, "Exact separated sampling: %d taps for ratio %d?", N, R);
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    // Row k: output pixels j with j % R == k. The tap n of such a pixel is
+    // texel `floor(at) - (N/2 - 1) + n`, at the distance compute_row() gives
+    // it for the subpixel offset `at - floor(at)`.
+    float weights[ORTHO_EXACT_MAX_VEC4 * 4] = {0};
+    float first[ORTHO_EXACT_MAX_RATIO];
+    for (int k = 0; k < R; k++) {
+        const double at = ex->origin + (double) k / R;
+        double base = floor(at), off = at - base;
+        if (off > 1.0 - 1e-9) {
+            base += 1.0; // `at` is a whole number that double could not hold
+            off = 0.0;
+        } else if (off < 1e-9) {
+            off = 0.0;
+        }
+
+        double w[4 * ORTHO_EXACT_MAX_VEC4], wsum = 0.0;
+        for (int n = 0; n < N; n++) {
+            w[n] = pl_filter_sample(&filt->params.config, n - (N / 2 - 1) - off);
+            wsum += w[n];
+        }
+
+        if (!(wsum > 0.0)) {
+            SH_FAIL(sh, "Exact separated sampling: weights sum to %f", wsum);
+            return false;
+        }
+
+        for (int n = 0; n < N; n++)
+            weights[k * S * 4 + n] = w[n] / wsum;
+        first[k] = base - (N / 2 - 1);
+    }
+
+    ident_t weights_v = sh_var(sh, (struct pl_shader_var) {
+        .var  = {
+            .name  = "phase_weights",
+            .type  = PL_VAR_FLOAT,
+            .dim_v = 4,
+            .dim_m = 1,
+            .dim_a = R * S,
+        },
+        .data = weights,
+    });
+
+    ident_t first_v = sh_var(sh, (struct pl_shader_var) {
+        .var  = {
+            .name  = "phase_first",
+            .type  = PL_VAR_FLOAT,
+            .dim_v = 1,
+            .dim_m = 1,
+            .dim_a = R,
+        },
+        .data = first,
+    });
+
+    ident_t origin_v = sh_var_float(sh, "phase_origin", ex->origin, false);
+
+    const bool horiz = ex->pass == SEP_HORIZ;
+    const char *type = sh_float_type(comps), *swiz = sh_swizzle(comps);
+    const bool use_ar = cfg->antiring > 0 && filt->radius != filt->radius_zero;
+
+    // m: the index of this output pixel along the axis, from the first one
+    GLSL("// pl_shader_sample_ortho (whole ratio, exact weights)          \n"
+         "vec4 color = vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);                          \n"
+         "{                                                               \n"
+         "vec2 pos = "$", pt = "$";                                       \n"
+         "vec2 size = vec2(textureSize("$", 0));                          \n"
+         "vec2 dir = vec2(%d.0, %d.0);                                    \n"
+         "float m = floor((dot(pos * size, dir) - 0.5 - "$") * %d.0 + 0.5); \n"
+         "float q = floor(m / %d.0);                                      \n"
+         "int k = clamp(int(m - q * %d.0), 0, %d);                        \n"
+         "float first = q + "$"[k] + 0.5;                                 \n"
+         "vec2 across = pos - dir * pos;                                  \n"
+         "vec4 ws;                                                        \n"
+         "%s c;                                                           \n"
+         "PRECISE %s ca = %s(0.0);                                        \n",
+         pos, pt, src_tex, horiz ? 1 : 0, horiz ? 0 : 1, origin_v, R, R, R,
+         R - 1, first_v, type, type, type);
+
+    if (use_ar) {
+        GLSL("%s hi = %s(0.0); \n"
+             "%s lo = %s(1e9); \n",
+             type, type, type, type);
+    }
+
+    for (int n = 0; n < N; n++) {
+        if (n % 4 == 0)
+            GLSL("ws = "$"[k * %d + %d]; \n", weights_v, S, n / 4);
+        GLSL("c = textureLod("$", across + dir * ((first + %d.0) * pt), 0.0).%s; \n",
+             src_tex, n, swiz);
+        if (use_ar && (n == N / 2 - 1 || n == N / 2))
+            GLSL("lo = min(lo, c); hi = max(hi, c); \n");
+        GLSL("ca += ws[%d] * c; \n", n % 4);
+    }
+
+    if (use_ar)
+        GLSL("ca = mix(ca, clamp(ca, lo, hi), "$"); \n", SH_FLOAT(cfg->antiring));
+
+    GLSL("color.%s = "$" * ca; \n"
+         "}                    \n",
+         swiz, SH_FLOAT(scale));
+    return true;
+}
+
 bool pl_shader_sample_ortho2(pl_shader sh, const struct pl_sample_src *src,
                              const struct pl_sample_filter_params *params)
 {
@@ -986,9 +1183,13 @@ bool pl_shader_sample_ortho2(pl_shader sh, const struct pl_sample_src *src,
                        src->rect.y1 == src->tex->params.h;
         }
     }
+    // An upscaling by a whole ratio takes its weights exactly, and its taps
+    // with nearest sampling: see ortho_exact_plan()
+    struct ortho_exact exact;
+    const bool use_exact = ortho_exact_plan(sh, src, &params->filter, &exact);
     if (!setup_src(sh, src, &src_tex, &pos, &pt,
-                   &ratio[SEP_HORIZ], &ratio[SEP_VERT],
-                   &comps, &scale, false, centered ? NEAREST : LINEAR))
+                   &ratio[SEP_HORIZ], &ratio[SEP_VERT], &comps, &scale, false,
+                   centered || use_exact ? NEAREST : LINEAR))
         return false;
 
 
@@ -1051,6 +1252,14 @@ bool pl_shader_sample_ortho2(pl_shader sh, const struct pl_sample_src *src,
         }
     }
 
+    if (use_exact) {
+        pl_assert(pass == exact.pass);
+        describe_filter(sh, &cfg, pass == SEP_HORIZ ? "ortho (horiz)" : "ortho (vert)",
+                        ratio[pass], ratio[pass]);
+        return ortho_exact_sample(sh, obj->filter, &cfg, &exact, comps, scale,
+                                  src_tex, pos, pt);
+    }
+
     int N = obj->filter->row_size; // number of samples to convolve
     int width = obj->filter->row_stride / 4; // width of the LUT texture
     ident_t lut = sh_lut(sh, sh_lut_params(
